#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb6340b-14ef-52dd-8c81-f42abe1f7db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173269} {:answer/id #uuid "d70c7e60-9413-5397-ad7d-cc813be7a7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173270} {:answer/id #uuid "d8172f30-0032-57c6-b711-70b10ccc26d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173271} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ab7837-d47d-5cb0-bc14-595dc1cfabaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173272} {:answer/id #uuid "4779d64a-2cc5-54b4-8a38-4f3be274cddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eb6340b-14ef-52dd-8c81-f42abe1f7db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173269} :answer-field/id #uuid "60c6ad40-5828-5d36-97ba-df0f631c5a76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5012f64-5f75-5c60-9687-8fa0fb8ccd67" :question/math-academy-id "q-102908" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $r=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $3,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/aa435c0ad4b4482c85a05af32afbbb7819b330a4292762ec66bad30a6ed31a44.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4d33523-ff13-58b8-a1eb-3e38090ce5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173287} {:answer/id #uuid "e631e2c3-5c34-5157-a4e1-58cfa0d132a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173288} {:answer/id #uuid "44cc7bfa-bba4-5fc5-98f2-69f6573198d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173289} {:answer/id #uuid "58c89805-2dd5-529d-8082-d97a4c931ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173290} {:answer/id #uuid "61565465-8038-580f-8dd2-d077a4f2acc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61565465-8038-580f-8dd2-d077a4f2acc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173291} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b23eeaa-526f-5ac3-b975-a98afdebb2db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e14b0f3-9fb0-5b36-9ab8-66e50f0fd598" :question/math-academy-id "q-102924" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $z=r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{r}=1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe35e88a-ac21-5d09-864b-5b7dac52b4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular sector" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324603} {:answer/id #uuid "b48bf848-c75a-51ad-8c63-85ecd27be008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324604} {:answer/id #uuid "4a202b4d-e096-5801-8a9d-f3af2131cbc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324605} {:answer/id #uuid "1e528de0-2028-5c8e-8b82-bed4e9a9c539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324606} {:answer/id #uuid "0718333a-9b84-52de-bb98-61017fd197fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b48bf848-c75a-51ad-8c63-85ecd27be008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324604} :answer-field/id #uuid "665e5a57-89fa-5faa-aa00-3080921957ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdc63001-5d2c-5b7d-9d01-a89ed92bf4ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-104231" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =5$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =5\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $5$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =5$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/a7e2f3f2ad24c353bc3dc63d82971b4888ccffc198479456beddd7fea185bd07.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de0c19f0-67cd-5fd4-9de9-e3b139a0472e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324610} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca12f86-20c8-5786-8a71-ae3f0f5beffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324611} {:answer/id #uuid "f95fb564-0626-5e59-9757-094c07ae709c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324612} {:answer/id #uuid "81e7d79e-6d0d-50ba-a9e2-44176d52d194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324613} {:answer/id #uuid "3b188870-9ae9-58f0-a27d-f54e32e59912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de0c19f0-67cd-5fd4-9de9-e3b139a0472e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324610} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac7644e-8d52-5ea5-9144-2f0e04f902b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f262fe7e-0560-56f7-ac9c-ac81b1d50660" :question/math-academy-id "q-104240" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals half the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abd168b4-e300-5c48-9370-4e183d0c38d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324617} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d41c70-8e3c-5308-a94d-4e0aa7e2bcc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=\\frac{{r}^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324618} {:answer/id #uuid "b73b9118-d2b4-5b02-910d-865b1a5f0e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\pi r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324619} {:answer/id #uuid "a271bdd0-e97e-5961-9bca-7d4290989adf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{r}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324620} {:answer/id #uuid "2a058c63-d6a7-5152-b924-b62dde912da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abd168b4-e300-5c48-9370-4e183d0c38d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324617} :answer-field/id #uuid "4984c0be-9153-5d1f-891f-21429c65a30e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6332f9d4-cc02-5d38-8ecf-e05dcc290462" :question/math-academy-id "q-104244" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/ee2764f5cf85f39107e458f347b048cc24227a55fae012b12f6cda4801cdfe78.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ z & =r\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r\\sqrt{3}$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}.$"}]}
